 "long_horizon_test": {
  "not_applicable": "not applicable: the data have no timestamps; each row is one user over one test period, so repeated campaigns, carry-over, wear-in and wear-out are not identified (recorded as limits). Every candidate's long-horizon measure is null and the E4 drift rule does not reject any candidate"
 },
 "identification_note": "Randomised eligibility (treatment) is the instrument for display (exposure). Assumptions: exclusion restriction (eligibility affects visits only through display), one-sided non-compliance (no control user is displayed: exposed_in_control = 0 in the conversion record) and monotonicity (trivial here). Under them, each stratum's complier baseline and complier visit rate under display are identified, and so is their log-odds difference. The score that defines strata uses pre-treatment features only and was fitted on the train split, so strata are balanced between the arms on the fit, validation and test splits.",
 "extrapolation_limits": "Criteo Uplift v2.1 (13,979,592 users of pooled randomised incrementality tests of Criteo display advertising; advertisers, countries and dates undisclosed; released 2018; sub-sampled non-uniformly by the publisher). The simulator may use this model for: the visit probability of an individual who is displayed (and, through ad_response@2, attends) an advertiser's display or feed campaign during a campaign period, as a log-odds shift of that individual's own organic visit probability. It may NOT infer: (1) effects for compliers unlike Criteo's - the displayed users were chosen by Criteo's bidder (exposure rises from about 0.1% to 15% of eligible users with the baseline score), so the effect is that of a targeted display on users a bidder reached; (2) effects of ad content, creative quality, price or relevance (not observed; ad_response@2 adds relevance and repetition only through a separate attention model and a declared assumption); (3) frequency, wear-in, wear-out, carry-over or repeated campaigns (no timestamps, one period per user); (4) negative effects (floored at 0); (5) absolute rates: the publisher's sub-sampling distorts levels; the log-odds shift is invariant to sub-sampling that depends on the outcome or on the arm only, not to sub-sampling that depends on exposure; (6) search advertising (a click on a sponsored result is a different decision). Strata are bins of a pre-treatment score; within-stratum heterogeneity is averaged, and the complier baseline of each stratum is a moment estimate (noisy where exposure is rare; clipped to [1e-4, 0.999]).",
 "feature_map": {
  "organic_logit": "log-odds of the displayed individual's organic (no-ad) probability of visiting the advertiser during the campaign period: in the data the stratum's complier baseline, in the simulator the individual's own organic visit probability (ad_response@2 input organic_visit_probability)"
 },
